  "worked_solution": "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}]}_{y=2}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\sqrt{x}({3}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.5) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0.5}{f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =[{0.5}^{3/2}]-[{0}^{3/2}] \\\\  & ≈0.3536.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
